Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Chrysalis of the Satellite
- Story & Context: Old Mamma Linh found the radio interference while tracking the North Star above the river, noting the smell of wet jasmine as the signal began to "sing" in a high-pitched, metallic frequency. She realized then that the metallic hum was a mirror of her own heartbeat, and she quickly looped the sound to keep the market boats from drifting apart in the fog. "The metal shell cracks to let the morning in," she whispered as the first pad chord bloomed, anchoring the entire fleet to the earth.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A macro photograph of a rusted, satellite-like metallic shell floating on dark, glassy water with a translucent moth emerging from its center; ethereal, cinematic, deep blues and silver highlights.

Track 2: Where the Larva Asks Why
- Story & Context: Young apprentice Hanh sat on the edge of the dock, questioning why the stars never descend to taste the river, her voice cracking as she confessed her fear of being left behind on the mudflats. She recorded her own doubt into the granular synth, letting the pitch-shifted echoes become a ghostly choir that responded to her loneliness with a heavy, cinematic drone. "Is the sky just a cage we forgot to unlatch?" she sang into the damp air, her voice buried deep under layers of thick, emotional cinepads.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A haunting, surreal image of a young woman sitting on a dilapidated wooden pier, her silhouette cast against a giant, glowing, amber-colored celestial sphere hanging just above the water level; painterly style with soft light flares.

Track 3: The Great Migration of the Silica
- Story & Context: Centuries later, the market elders tell the myth of the "Silica Migration," claiming that all the sound ever generated in these boats eventually turns into glass dust that settles on the riverbed. They point to the shimmering, reflective silt beneath the water as proof that their music has physically reshaped the landscape of the market. "We are the dust that remembers the flight," the chorus of the elders hums in a final, dissonant resolution, marking the moment the music ceases to be sound and becomes permanent memory.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A breathtaking underwater view of a riverbed covered in glowing, crystalline sand particles that are rising toward the surface like stars moving in reverse; high-contrast, mystical, deep indigo and radiant gold colors.

